# What ndusr may open once it stops being root.
#
# SECURITY-PLAN.md section 5 Phase 1, and the table in SECURITY-AUDIT.md
# section 6: every device the UI touches, and the group that grants it.
# Most of them need nothing here -- eudev's own 50-udev-default.rules already
# puts /dev/fb0 in video, /dev/input/event* in input, /dev/snd/* in audio and
# /dev/ttyUSB* in dialout, all at 0660. Those four are the common case and
# they are already right.
#
# This file is the remainder: the nodes and attributes with no stock rule,
# which are therefore root:root 0600 (a device node with no GROUP= gets 0600
# from eudev, not 0660) or root-owned 0644 in sysfs.
#
# NOTHING HERE CHANGES ANYTHING TODAY. Every process on the phone is root and
# root ignores all of it. It is here so that the users exist, the groups are
# granted and the rules are proven before anything drops privilege -- which
# is the order SECURITY-PLAN.md asks for, and the opposite of discovering on
# a phone that the keypad stopped working.
#
# A wrong group name fails as a permission problem rather than a build error:
# eudev logs one line and falls back to gid 0, which also drops the node to
# 0600. Every group named below is created by neodct/configs/users-table.txt.

# --- i2c: the keypad expander and the fuel gauge -------------------------
#
# The one device class with no stock rule at all, and the one the audit
# predicted would bite. /dev/i2c-3 carries the PCF8575 the keypad matrix
# hangs off (nd_pcf8575.c, nd_keypadsetup.c) and the MAX17048 fuel gauge
# (nd_battery.c). Without this it is root:root 0600 and a non-root UI has no
# keys and no battery reading.
#
# Not granted to ndusr_ut. An i2c bus is a bus: anything on it is reachable
# by address, and "the browser can talk to the fuel gauge" is not a sentence
# with a good ending.
SUBSYSTEM=="i2c-dev", GROUP="i2c", MODE="0660"

# ============ THE QUOTING RULES, WHICH ARE NOT THE SHELL'S ============
#
# Two different parsers read a RUN+= value, one after the other, and they do
# not agree with each other. Both are in eudev 3.2.14:
#
#   get_key()          src/udev/udev-rules.c -- reads the RULE. A value runs
#                      from one double quote to the next; \" inside it is
#                      honoured and does not end the value. Line continuation
#                      is backslash-newline, checked as line[len-2].
#
#   udev_build_argv()  src/udev/udev-event.c -- splits that value into ARGV,
#                      and it has NO escapes whatsoever. It walks the string;
#                      a quote character, single or double, starts an element
#                      that ends at the next one of the same kind; anything
#                      else is split on spaces.
#
# So the rule to follow is the second parser's, because it is the stricter
# one: the script handed to `sh -c` must contain no quote character at all.
# There is nothing to escape it WITH. One apostrophe inside the script ends
# the argv element in the middle of itself, and the remainder becomes further
# arguments that sh never looks at -- with no error, because both parsers
# think they succeeded.
#
# Hence single quotes around the script and none inside it. Every path below
# comes from sysfs and has no spaces in it, so nothing inside needs quoting
# anyway.
#
# $$ is how a literal $ reaches the shell. A bare $ before a name eudev does
# not recognise is copied through, so $b would happen to work today -- but
# $id, $name, $links, $result and a dozen more ARE substitutions, and a
# shell variable is one rename away from becoming one.
#
# A syntax error here costs this rule and not the file: parse_file() ignores
# what add_rule() returns and reads the next line. Smaller blast radius than
# it first looks, and still not one worth spending.

# --- backlight, the PWM tier ----------------------------------------------
#
# nd_backlight.c writes brightness. A udev rule is the ordinary way to hand
# an attribute to a group; the alternative is a setuid helper for something
# that is one integer.
#
# This covers ONE of nd_backlight.c's two tiers, and on this board it is the
# tier that does not exist. /sys/class/backlight is populated by a pwm-backlight
# node in the device tree; nd_fb.h records that the panel's BL wire needs pwm9
# there, that the DTB lives in the boot partition, and that an .ndsw update
# replaces the rootfs and never the boot partition -- so a phone that has only
# ever been updated over the air has an empty /sys/class/backlight and drives
# the panel through /sys/class/gpio instead. See S90display for that half: the
# gpio class has no uevent for an exported pin, so no rule here can grant it
# and a boot script has to.
SUBSYSTEM=="backlight", ACTION=="add", \
    RUN+="/bin/sh -c 'b=/sys%p/brightness; [ -e $$b ] && chgrp video $$b && chmod 0664 $$b'"

# --- backlight, the GPIO tier ---------------------------------------------
#
# Belt to S90display's braces, and it is the weaker of the two on purpose.
#
# The sysfs GPIO class is not device-shaped. Writing a pin number to
# /sys/class/gpio/export creates gpioN/ SYNCHRONOUSLY, inside that write(2),
# and emits NO uevent at all -- so there is never an `add` for udev to match
# and a rule keyed on the exported pin can never fire. What DOES generate an
# add is the gpiochip, and only for the controller.
#
# So this grants what it can reach -- a gpiochipN's own attributes -- and the
# pin nd_backlight.c actually writes is granted by S90display, which exports it
# and then chgrps it, in that order, because only the exporter knows when the
# directory appeared. A rule that silently matches nothing is exactly the shape
# the cpufreq comment below warns about, and this one is documented as such
# rather than mistaken for coverage.
SUBSYSTEM=="gpio", ACTION=="add", \
    RUN+="/bin/sh -c 'for f in value direction; do p=/sys%p/$$f; [ -e $$p ] && { chgrp video $$p; chmod 0664 $$p; }; done'"

# --- cpufreq --------------------------------------------------------------
#
# nd_cpufreq.c sets scaling_min_freq and scaling_max_freq -- the phone drops
# its ceiling on the home screen and raises it for Koki. cpufreq attributes
# are not a udev subsystem event on every kernel, so this hangs off the cpu
# subsystem's add and checks for the directory rather than assuming it.
#
# The `[ -e ]` is not about log noise -- a RUN child's stderr goes to
# /dev/null unless udevd is at LOG_INFO or above, and a non-zero exit is
# logged at debug and otherwise discarded, so a rule that fails on every boot
# fails SILENTLY. It is there because not every kernel exports all three
# attributes: QEMU's virt machine has no cpufreq directory at all, and a
# kernel built with one governor has no scaling_governor. Testing for each
# one is what makes the rule mean the same thing on both.
SUBSYSTEM=="cpu", ACTION=="add", \
    RUN+="/bin/sh -c 'for f in scaling_min_freq scaling_max_freq scaling_governor; do p=/sys%p/cpufreq/$$f; [ -e $$p ] && { chgrp ndusr $$p; chmod 0664 $$p; }; done'"

# --- uinput ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# apps/Browser/main.c synthesises a uinput device so netsurf-fb sees the
# arrow keys the core chose to give it and nothing else. The core is what
# opens /dev/uinput, so this grants the group the core will run as -- not
# ndusr_ut, which must keep receiving keys rather than injecting them.
#
# ndusr, therefore, and NOT `input`. This rule said GROUP="input" and the
# comment above it said why that would be wrong, which is the whole lesson:
# ndusr_ut is in `input`, and has to be, because that is how it RECEIVES keys
# from /dev/input/event*. Granting uinput to the same group hands the
# untrusted set key INJECTION as well, and those two are not the same
# privilege at all.
#
# What it would have cost: the browser owns the whole screen while it is up --
# there is no compositor on a 64 MB phone, see SECURITY.md -- and a process
# that can both draw the UI and type into it can drive the real one. It cannot
# open the modem; it would not need to. It could synthesise the keys that make
# the PHONE open the modem, which is SECURITY-AUDIT.md section 4 Q1 reached
# the long way round.
#
# Found by nd-selftest on the first real boot, not by reading this file.
KERNEL=="uinput", GROUP="ndusr", MODE="0660"

# --- rtc ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# nd_clock.c sets the hardware clock after an SNTP sync. The stock rules
# leave /dev/rtc0 root:root 0600. This is the smaller half of the problem --
# the other half is clock_settime(), which is CAP_SYS_TIME and cannot be
# given away with a mode bit; see SECURITY-PLAN.md section 5 Phase 1 on
# preferring an auditable helper to a capability on the whole core.
KERNEL=="rtc[0-9]*", GROUP="ndusr", MODE="0660"

# --- ALSA capture ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# The uinput lesson again, in the other direction, and it took an audit to
# see it: ndusr_ut is in `audio` because that is how the browser and the
# media player PLAY sound, and eudev's stock rule puts every /dev/snd node in
# `audio` at 0660 -- capture devices included. So the same group membership
# that lets a web page play a video lets it open the microphone.
#
# Playing and recording are no more the same privilege than receiving and
# injecting keys were. The `c` suffix is ALSA's own: pcmC0D0p is playback,
# pcmC0D0c is capture, and the only things on this phone that legitimately
# record are the modem's voice bridge (nd_modem_audio.c) and MicTest -- the
# core and a stock app, both ndusr.
#
# Playback nodes are deliberately left alone. Taking `audio` off ndusr_ut
# entirely would be the blunter fix and it would silence the browser, which
# is a feature nobody asked to remove.
SUBSYSTEM=="sound", KERNEL=="pcmC[0-9]*D[0-9]*c", GROUP="ndusr", MODE="0660"
